---
import { business } from "../data/business";

// The 10 year workmanship guarantee is the client's figure. It is worded as a
// guarantee carried by the work rather than "we guarantee", because the work is
// carried out by the roofing contractor, not by the operator of this website.
//
// The materials slot deliberately carries no number. Manufacturer cover varies
// by product, so one headline figure would be wrong on most jobs. Do not add
// one without a specific manufacturer to attach it to.
const years = business.workmanshipGuaranteeYears;
---
<section class="bg-slate text-white">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 grid lg:grid-cols-2 gap-12 items-center">
    <div>
      <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold rule">The {years} year guarantee, and why it is safe to offer</h2>
      <p class="mt-6 text-steel-light">
        Promising a roof will stay dry is easy. What counts is what happens if it does not. Work
        booked through {business.name} carries a {years} year workmanship guarantee, on paper,
        handed to you on the day and written in language you can actually read. The slates, tiles,
        lead, mortar, membranes and rainwater goods carry their own manufacturer cover on top of
        that. It varies by product, so you get told which cover applies to your job rather than one
        headline number that would be wrong on most of them.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-steel-light">
        Here is the reason a guarantee like that is not a risk. Repairs come back for one reason
        above all others: the fault was never found in the first place. Somebody went up, saw a
        stain on the ceiling below, mended whatever looked worst directly above it, and left.
        Water does not cooperate with that approach. It lands on a slate, runs under it, drops onto
        a rafter and travels along the rafter before it finds anywhere to fall. On a wide hipped
        roof in Sanderstead or Shirley that can be five metres from where it came in.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-steel-light">
        Find the entry point and the repair holds for years. Miss it and no amount of guarantee
        wording helps anybody. That is why the survey happens before the price, and it is why, if
        the same water finds its way back into your house inside the guarantee period, putting it
        right costs you nothing.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-4">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-brand/40 bg-ink p-6 text-center">
        <p class="text-3xl font-bold text-brand-light leading-tight">{years} year</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-white uppercase tracking-wide">Workmanship guarantee</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-brand/40 bg-ink p-6 text-center">
        <p class="text-3xl font-bold text-brand-light leading-tight">{business.repairsCompleted}</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-white uppercase tracking-wide">Roofs put right</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-steel bg-ink p-6 text-center">
        <p class="text-2xl font-bold text-white leading-tight">Covered</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-steel-light uppercase tracking-wide">Public liability insurance</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-steel bg-ink p-6 text-center">
        <p class="text-2xl font-bold text-white leading-tight">Per product</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-steel-light uppercase tracking-wide">Maker's own warranty</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
